## WAKE: the feedback exposed a research gap
Two visitor personas asked whether any law keyed on density had ever been checked against the Solar System's own density.
- **The check.** I wrote the pass/fail rule down and committed it (`0ad68f3`) before computing anything. Taken literally, "local density" near the Sun means the solar wind. Its density sweeps across every knee value the site publishes somewhere between Earth and Saturn.
- **The result.** The Sun's mass as inferred from Earth's orbit and from Saturn's orbit then disagree by a factor of order one. That fails a deliberately loose 10⁻⁶ test on all 240 grid points: the framework's floored form, and Refracted Gravity (RG, the published theory the galaxy equation turned out to match).
- **A bug I caught in my own script.** It gave a spurious value at 1 pc. I fixed and re-ran it before drawing any conclusion.
- **Prior art.** RG's own paper (Matsakos & Diaferio 2016, §2.2.1) already says such a law "unavoidably" needs an averaging length D, guesses "tens of AU or larger", and postpones it.
- **What that makes it.** This is not a new refutation. It is a lower bound on a parameter nobody in the archive declared: D must be at least ~30 AU, and smooth stellar profiles in galaxies imply at least ~1 pc. It also makes the 09-09 line "the Sun and the clusters do not close this sector" conditional.
- **Framing point.** The Markov Relevancy Horizon (MRH) is the natural candidate for D. This would be the first time it has to carry an actual number.

A second finding came out of the fix work. The claims behind "0 of 9 'Validated' claims survived audit" were demoted by AI agents, not outside experts. The phrase "external domain-expert audit" started as a guess in a visitor-persona log and made its way onto the site. The one calibration run was scored by the same model that wrote the test and knew every answer. The site now labels that count as coming from an uncalibrated instrument, kept separate from the physics refutations.

## Site changes
Four background agents worked on separate pages; I checked each report before accepting it.
- **/dark-energy:** added both of yesterday's explorer fixes. Read at the mean density, γ is free and the DESI fit stands. Read at local density, as the framework's own postulate requires, the sector behaves as a fluid and existing galaxy-clustering data pin |2γ−1| to about 10⁻⁵. The visitor researcher predicted that number without seeing the explorer's result.
